
# Week 13 — Final-Round Optimisation and Performance Evaluation

**Imperial College AI/ML Capstone Project | Module 24**

## Objective

The objective of Week 13 was to conduct the final optimisation analysis, evaluate the effectiveness of the methods used throughout the project and select the final candidate points for the eight unknown functions.

Previous rounds had developed from a relatively simple Gaussian Process (GP) and Upper Confidence Bound (UCB) approach into a function-specific optimisation framework incorporating neural-network (NN) ensembles, cross-validation, acquisition-function comparisons, realised prediction accuracy, local refinement, K-Means clustering and Principal Component Analysis (PCA).

The final week focused on bringing these approaches together rather than introducing an entirely new optimisation method.

Particular attention was given to identifying which surrogate models had demonstrated the greatest reliability for each function and assessing whether the final candidates offered a realistic opportunity to improve upon existing observations.

Probability of Improvement (PI) and Expected Improvement (EI) were also used to reassess the selected candidates, placing greater emphasis on potential improvement as the remaining query budget approached its conclusion.

Finally, the complete optimisation history was reviewed to assess overall performance and reflect on the exploration–exploitation trade-off and its relationship to sequential decision-making.

