### 3.2 Gaussian Process Kernel Behaviour

The Matérn kernel allowed separate length scales across input dimensions.

This differed from the earlier isotropic RBF approach and provided greater flexibility in representing functions that varied more strongly along particular dimensions.

However, several fitted length scales reached the upper bound of 1000.

This occurred in Functions 1, 2, 3, 5, 7 and 8.

Such results suggested that the model was treating certain dimensions as relatively insensitive within the observed data.

They did not necessarily indicate that the fitted model was unusable, but remained important diagnostics when interpreting its predictions.

Functions 4 and 6 fitted without reported convergence warnings in this final diagnostic stage.

Overall, the updated GP performed reasonably well for most functions, although the fitted kernel parameters and validation results continued to justify function-specific caution.

